  narrativeContent: `For most of the eighteenth century the Northwest Coast was the last blank on European maps of the world. Spain claimed it by papal decree, Russia approached it along the Aleutians, Britain and France speculated about a Northwest Passage opening onto it, and the nations who lived there, the Tlingit, Haida, Tsimshian, Nuu-chah-nulth, Coast Salish and Chinook, had no reason to think any of that mattered. Between 1741 and 1846 it came to matter very much.

## The Russians come east

Vitus Bering, a Dane in Russian service, reached Alaska in 1741 and died on the way home, but his crew brought back sea-otter pelts, and the otter's fur was worth more in China than anything else on earth. Russian hunters moved east along the Aleutians, forcing the Aleut to hunt for them. Grigory Shelikhov planted the first permanent settlement at Kodiak in 1784; his manager Alexander Baranov pushed the hunt to Sitka, fought the Tlingit for it in 1802 and 1804, and made New Archangel the capital of a Russian America that lasted until 1867.

## Spain reacts

Alarmed by the Russians, Spain sent Juan Pérez north from Mexico in 1774. He traded with the Haida off Dixon Entrance and lay off Nootka Sound, the first European to see the coast of British Columbia. More Spanish voyages followed, and Spanish names remain on the map from the Strait of Juan de Fuca to Galiano Island. But Spain's claim rested on a decree, not on ships or settlers.

## Cook, Canton and the fur rush

James Cook spent a month at Yuquot in Maquinna's territory in 1778 on his way to search for the Northwest Passage from the Pacific side. His crews traded for sea-otter skins almost by accident; when they sold them in Canton the next year for a hundred times their cost, the news set off a rush. British, American and other traders came for furs through the 1780s and 1790s. They needed the chiefs, Maquinna at Nootka and Wickaninnish at Clayoquot, who controlled the trade, fed the ships and set the prices, and the chiefs used the rivalry to grow richer and stronger.

## The Nootka Crisis

In 1789 Spain sent Esteban José Martínez to occupy Nootka Sound. He built Fort San Miguel, seized the ships of John Meares's company, and days later one of his sailors shot the Mowachaht chief Callicum dead as he protested from his canoe. Meares went home and told Parliament a dramatic and exaggerated story; Britain mobilized its fleet; and Spain, without an ally, gave way. The Nootka Convention of 1790 opened the coast north of Spain's settlements to British trade. George Vancouver came in 1792 to receive the Spanish surrender at Nootka and to chart the whole coast from California to Alaska in three summers of boat work, including the sound his lieutenant Peter Puget surveyed. He missed the Columbia; the Boston trader Robert Gray entered it two weeks later and named it for his ship.

## Over the mountains

While the ships worked the coast, the fur trade came over the Rockies. Lewis and Clark reached the Columbia's mouth for the United States in 1805. Simon Fraser and John Stuart built the North West Company's posts in New Caledonia in 1806 and descended the Fraser in 1808. David Thompson crossed Howse Pass in 1807, and when the Piikani closed it, crossed Athabasca Pass in the winter of 1811, mapping the whole Columbia on the way. John Jacob Astor's Pacific Fur Company built Astoria at the river's mouth in 1811, lost the Tonquin at Clayoquot and sold out to the North West Company in 1813. The posts that followed, Fort Okanogan, Kamloops, Spokane House, Fort Nez Percés, were linked by brigade trails and the river.

## The Hudson's Bay Company's Pacific

After the 1821 merger the Hudson's Bay Company governed everything from Russian America to California from Fort Vancouver, under John McLoughlin. It farmed the Willamette and Puget Sound, sent Peter Skene Ogden to trap the Snake Country bare so that American trappers would find nothing, leased the Alaska panhandle from the Russians in 1839, and sold furs in London and salmon in Hawaii. It also depended, at every post, on Indigenous and Métis families: Kwah's Dakelh at Fort St. James, Comcomly's Chinook at the Columbia's mouth, and women like Amelia Douglas, who saved her husband's life at Fort St. James in 1828 and later presided as the governor's wife at Victoria.

## Drawing the line

American settlers poured into the Willamette over the Oregon Trail in the 1840s, and the United States demanded the whole country to Alaska. The Oregon Treaty of 1846 split it along the 49th parallel to the coast and down the Strait of Juan de Fuca; Fort Vancouver, Fort Nisqually and the Columbia were lost, and the company moved its headquarters to Fort Victoria, which James Douglas had founded in 1843. Russia sold Alaska to the United States in 1867. The border that runs through the Salish Sea today was drawn by fur traders' posts and explorers' rivers as much as by diplomats.`,
